<div class="page">
  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>Deductions</h2>
    <p>Compensation for Services. Generally, you must include in gross income everything you receive in payment for personal services. This includes wages, salaries, commissions, fees, and tips. You must also include any compensation such as fringe benefits and stock options.</p>
    <p>You need not receive the compensation in cash for it to be taxable. Payments you receive in the form of goods or services generally must be included in gross income if they have a fair market value.</p>
    <p>You must include in your income all unemployment compensation you receive. See Publication <span data-field="publication_number_4">{{ publication_number_4 }}</span>, Taxable and Nontaxable Income, for more detailed information on specific types of income.</p>

    <h2>Volunteer work.</h2>
    <p>Do not include in your gross income amounts you receive for volunteer services or any reimbursement for out-of-pocket expenses under any of the following programs:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Retirement Volunteer Program (RSVP)</li>
      <li>Foster Grandparent Program</li>
      <li>Senior Companion Program</li>
      <li>Service Corps of Retired Executives (SCORE)</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Retirement Plan Distributions</h2>
    <p>This section summarizes the tax treatment of amounts you receive from certain retirement plans. These plans include employer-sponsored plans and annuities, and disability income benefits. See Publication <span data-field="publication_number_5">{{ publication_number_5 }}</span>, Individual Retirement Arrangements (IRAs), Including Roth IRAs and Education Savings Plans, and Publication <span data-field="publication_number_6">{{ publication_number_6 }}</span>, Pension and Annuity Income.</p>

    <h2>Individual Retirement Arrangements (IRAs)</h2>
    <p>In general, include distributions from a traditional IRA in your gross income in the year you receive them. A Roth IRA is not a Roth, SIMPLE, or education IRA. Exceptions to the general rule are provided in Publication <span data-field="publication_number_7">{{ publication_number_7 }}</span>, and the return of nondeductible contributions. These tax-concepts are discussed in Publication <span data-field="publication_number_8">{{ publication_number_8 }}</span>.</p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>If you made nondeductible contributions to a traditional IRA, you must file Form <span data-field="form_number_2">{{ form_number_2 }}</span> if you want to track these contributions. You may have to pay a $50 penalty. Also, when you make withdrawals from your traditional IRA, the amounts will be taxable to you. You can see more information on nondeductible contributions in Publication <span data-field="publication_number_9">{{ publication_number_9 }}</span>.</p>
    </div>
    <p>For more information on IRA distributions, see Publication <span data-field="publication_number_10">{{ publication_number_10 }}</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="two-column-layout">
    <div class="column">
      <h2>Itemized Deductions</h2>
      <p>Generally, personal deductions (other than those listed below) are not deductible. You can only deduct certain expenses, such as medical expenses, state and local taxes, and mortgage interest. You must itemize your deductions to claim them.</p>
      <p>For more information on itemized deductions, see Publication <span data-field="publication_number_11">{{ publication_number_11 }}</span>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="column">
      <h2>Medical and Dental Expenses</h2>
      <p>You can only deduct medical and dental expenses that exceed a certain percentage of your adjusted gross income. The percentage changes each year. For more information, see Publication <span data-field="publication_number_12">{{ publication_number_12 }}</span>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>Credit for the Elderly or the Disabled</h2>
    <p>You may be eligible for this credit if you meet certain age and income requirements. The requirements change annually. For more information, see Publication <span data-field="publication_number_13">{{ publication_number_13 }}</span>.</p>

    <h2>Child and Dependent Care Credit</h2>
    <p>You may be eligible for this credit if you have dependents who require care so that you can work or look for work. The requirements change annually. For more information, see Publication <span data-field="publication_number_14">{{ publication_number_14 }}</span>.</p>

    <h2>Earned Income Credit</h2>
    <p>See the section on Earned Income Credit on page <span data-field="page_number_1">{{ page_number_1 }}</span> for more information.</p>

    <h2>Estimated Tax</h2>
    <p>If you expect to owe at least $1,000 in taxes for the year, you may need to pay estimated tax payments. The due dates and amounts change annually. For more information, see Publication <span data-field="publication_number_15">{{ publication_number_15 }}</span>.</p>

    <h2>How To Get More Information</h2>
    <p>For more information on any of the topics discussed in this publication, see the IRS website or contact the IRS directly. The IRS website is <span data-field="irs_website">{{ irs_website }}</span>.</p>
  </div>
</div>

