<div class="page">
  <h2>Itemized Deductions</h2>
  <p>Some individuals should itemize their deductions because it will save them money. Otherwise, they should use the standard deduction. See the discussion under Standard Deduction, earlier in this publication, to determine if it would be to your advantage to itemize deductions.</p>

  <h3>Medical and Dental Expenses</h3>
  <p>You may deduct expenses for medical and dental expenses, some taxes, certain interest expenses, charitable contributions, certain losses, and other miscellaneous expenses may be itemized as deductions on Schedule A (Form 1040).</p>
  <p>You may be subject to a limit on some of your itemized deductions. See the discussion under Standard Deduction, earlier in this publication, to determine if it would be to your advantage to itemize deductions.</p>

  <h3>Medical Insurance Premiums</h3>
  <p>You can include in medical expenses insurance premiums you pay for policies that cover medical care. Policies can provide for:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Hospitalization, surgical fees, X-rays, etc.</li>
    <li>Prescription drugs.</li>
    <li>Replacement of lost or damaged contact lenses.</li>
    <li>Qualified long-term care.</li>
    <li>Membership in an association that gives cooperation for medical care, medical service, or group hospitalization and clinical care.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If you have a policy that provides more than one kind of payment, you can include the premiums for the medical care part of the policy if the charge for the medical part is reasonable. The cost of the medical portion must be separately stated in the insurance contract or given to you in a separate statement.</p>
  <p><strong>Medicare A.</strong> If you are covered under social security (or if you are a government employee who paid Medicare tax), you are enrolled in Medicare A. The payroll tax paid for Medicare A is not a medical expense. If you are not covered under Medicare A, you may deduct the government employee who paid Medicare tax, you can deduct the premiums paid for Medicare A. In this situation the premiums paid in 1999 for Medicare A can be included as a medical expense.</p>

