<div class="page">
  <h2>Qualified Individual</h2>
  <p>You are a qualified individual for this credit if you are a U.S. citizen or resident at the end of the tax year, and:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Age 65 or older, or</li>
    <li>Under 65, retired on permanent and total disability, and</li>
    <li>Received taxable disability income, and</li>
    <li>Did not reach mandatory retirement age (defined later under Disability Income).</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Age 65.</strong> You are considered to be age 65 on the date of your 65th birthday. Therefore, you are age 65 at the end of the year if your 65th birthday is on January 1 of the following year.</p>

  <p><strong>U.S. citizen or resident.</strong> You must be a U.S. citizen or resident at the end of the tax year. If you are a nonresident alien at any time during the tax year and were married to a U.S. citizen or resident at the end of the tax year, you must file Form 1040. If you are a nonresident alien and your spouse is a U.S. resident, you must file Form 1040. If you are a nonresident alien and your spouse is not a U.S. resident, you must file Form 1040A.</p>

  <p><strong>Married persons.</strong> Generally, if you are married at the end of the tax year, you must file a joint return to take the credit. However, if you and your spouse did not live in the same household at any time during the tax year, you can file either a joint return or separate returns and still take the credit.</p>

  <p><strong>Head of household.</strong> You can file as head of house-hold and qualify to take the credit even if your spouse lived with you during the first 6 months of the year if you meet all of the test. See Publication 524 and Publication 501.</p>

  <p><strong>Under age 65.</strong> If you are under age 65, you can qualify for the credit only if you are retired on permanent and total disability. You are retired on permanent and total disability if:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>You were permanently and totally disabled when you retired, and</li>
    <li>You retired on disability before the end of the tax year.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Even if you do not retire formally, you are considered retired on permanent and total disability if you have stopped working because of your disability. You are considered retired on permanent and total disability if you cannot engage in any substantial gainful activity because of your physical or mental condition. A physician must certify that the condition has lasted or can be expected to last contin-</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <p>the work. The performance of these duties does not, of itself, show that Joan is able to engage in substantial gainful activity.</p>

  <h2>Substantial Gainful Activity.</h2>
  <p>Substantial gainful activity. Substantial gainful activity is the performance of significant duties over a reasonable period of time while working for pay or profit, or in work generally done for pay or profit. Full-time work (or part-time work done at the end of the tax year) is considered to be substantial gainful activity if the amount of work is such that you are able to engage in substantial gainful activity. This is true even if you are not paid or if you are paid less than the minimum wage. But the act of work itself, not the amount of work, is what matters. The fact that you have not worked for some time is not a factor. The fact that you have not worked for some time is not a factor. The fact that you have not worked for some time is not a factor.</p>
  <p><strong>Example 1.</strong> Trish, a sales clerk, retired on disability. She was able to work for a few hours a week before she retired. She was able to work for a few hours a week after she retired. She is able to engage in substantial gainful activity. This is true even if she is able to engage in substantial gainful activity.</p>
  <p><strong>Example 2.</strong> John, who retired on disability, took a job with a former employer on a trial basis. The purpose of the job was to see if he could return to work. He was able to work for a few hours a week. He is able to engage in substantial gainful activity. This is true even if he is able to engage in substantial gainful activity.</p>
  <p><strong>Example 3.</strong> John, who retired on disability, took a job with a former employer on a trial basis. The purpose of the job was to see if he could return to work. He was able to work for a few hours a week. He is able to engage in substantial gainful activity. This is true even if he is able to engage in substantial gainful activity.</p>
  <p><strong>Example 4.</strong> Joan, who retired on disability from employment as a bookkeeper, lives with her sister who is a homemaker. Joan assists her sister for a few hours a week with housekeeping, washing dishes, answering phones, registering guests, and other household tasks. Joan is able to engage in substantial gainful activity. This is true even if she is able to engage in substantial gainful activity.</p>

  <h2>Income Limits</h2>
  <p>To determine if you qualify for the credit, you must consider two income limits. The first limit is the amount of your adjusted gross income (AGI). The second limit is the amount of nontaxable social security or other nontaxable pension(s) is equal to or more than.</p>
  <p>If the amount of your AGI and nontaxable pension(s) is equal to or more than the income limits, you may be able to claim the credit.</p>

  <h2>Figuring the Credit</h2>
  <p>If you figure the credit yourself, fill out the front of either Schedule R (if you are filing Form 1040) or Schedule R (if you are filing Form 1040A). Next, fill out Part III of either Schedule R or Schedule R.</p>
  <p>There are four steps in Part III to determine the amount of your credit.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Determine your initial amounts (lines 10-12).</li>
    <li>Determine your nontaxable social security and certain other nontaxable pension(s) and disability benefits you received (lines 13a, 13b, and 13c).</li>
    <li>Determine your excess adjusted gross income (lines 14-17).</li>
    <li>Determine your credit (lines 18-20).</li>
  </ol>
  <p>For more information on these steps, get Publication 524.</p>

  <h2>Limits on Credit</h2>
  <p>The amount of your credit may be limited if:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>You file Form 2441, Child and Dependent Care Expenses.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Be sure to read your form instructions before claiming your credit.</p>

  <h2>Child and Dependent Care Credit</h2>
  <p>You may be able to claim the credit if you pay someone to care for your dependent child who is under age 13 or for your spouse or dependent who is not able to care for himself or herself. The credit can be up to 30% of your expenses. To qualify, you must pay these expenses so you can work or look for work.</p>
